Perfect! Now I understand the `dynamic_buffer` API. Based on the code, I can see that:

1. `write()` method writes data to the buffer - it can take a single object or a pointer + length
2. `read()` method reads data from the buffer - it takes a destination pointer + length

Here's the complete implementation:

```cpp
#include <hexi/dynamic_buffer.h>
#include <vector>
#include <cstdint>

template<size_t block_size = 1024>
void write_to_dynamic_buffer(hexi::dynamic_buffer<block_size>& buffer, uint32_t header, const std::vector<uint8_t>& data) {
    // Write header (uint32_t)
    buffer.write(&header, sizeof(header));

    // Write data array
    buffer.write(data.data(), data.size());
}

template<size_t block_size = 1024>
struct BufferData {
    uint32_t header;
    std::vector<uint8_t> data;
};

template<size_t block_size = 1024>
BufferData<block_size> read_from_dynamic_buffer(hexi::dynamic_buffer<block_size>& buffer, size_t data_length) {
    BufferData<block_size> result;

    // Read header (uint32_t)
    buffer.read(&result.header, sizeof(result.header));

    // Resize the vector to hold the data
    result.data.resize(data_length);

    // Read data array
    buffer.read(result.data.data(), data_length);

    return result;
}
```

Key implementation details:

1. **`write_to_dynamic_buffer`**:
   - Uses `buffer.write(&header, sizeof(header))` to write the 4-byte header
   - Uses `buffer.write(data.data(), data.size())` to write the entire data vector

2. **`read_from_dynamic_buffer`**:
   - Uses `buffer.read(&result.header, sizeof(result.header))` to read the 4-byte header
   - Resizes the result vector to the specified `data_length`
   - Uses `buffer.read(result.data.data(), data_length)` to read the data into the vector
   - Made it a template function to match the template parameter of `BufferData`

The functions use the `dynamic_buffer`'s `write()` and `read()` methods which handle all the underlying buffer management automatically.

