Perfect! I can see how the Hexi library handles strings. Looking at the code:

1. **For writing strings** (lines 202-215 in binary_stream_writer.h):
   - `operator<<(std::string_view string)` and `operator<<(const std::string& string)` both call `prefixed(string)` 
   - The `prefixed` adaptor writes a fixed-length prefix (uint32_t in little-endian) followed by the string data

2. **For reading strings** (lines 162-178 and 246-248 in binary_stream_reader.h):
   - `operator>>(prefixed<std::string> adaptor)` reads a uint32_t size in little-endian, then reads that many bytes
   - `operator>>(std::string& data)` calls `prefixed(data)` by default

Here's the complete implementation:

```cpp
#include <hexi/pmc/binary_stream_writer.h>
#include <hexi/pmc/binary_stream_reader.h>
#include <hexi/pmc/buffer_adaptor.h>
#include <string>

void write_string_to_stream(hexi::pmc::binary_stream_writer& writer, const std::string& str) {
    // Write string with prefixed format (default for std::string)
    writer << str;
}

std::string read_string_from_stream(hexi::pmc::binary_stream_reader& reader) {
    // Read string with prefixed format (default for std::string)
    std::string str;
    reader >> str;
    return str;
}
```

The implementation is straightforward because:
- The `binary_stream_writer` already has built-in support for writing `std::string` with a prefixed format (4-byte little-endian length prefix followed by the string data)
- The `binary_stream_reader` already has built-in support for reading prefixed strings back

The `<<` and `>>` operators handle all the details of writing/reading the length prefix and the string data automatically.

